
This directory is intended for PlatformIO Test Runner and project tests.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html

Localization host test:

    g++ -std=c++17 -Isrc test/localization_ekf_test.cpp \
        src/domain/localization/localization_ekf.cpp \
        src/domain/localization/localization_preprocessor.cpp \
        src/domain/localization/localization.cpp \
        -o .pio/localization_ekf_test.exe
    .pio/localization_ekf_test.exe

The test covers signed forward/reverse velocity, the east-zero and
counter-clockwise yaw convention, the pi boundary, gyro-bias convergence,
GPS and magnetic outlier rejection, delayed-GNSS replay, and covariance
symmetry/positivity.

Stack and flip detector host tests:

    g++ -std=c++17 -Isrc test/stuck_detector_test.cpp \
        src/domain/motion/stuck_detector.cpp \
        -o .pio/stuck_detector_test.exe
    .pio/stuck_detector_test.exe

    g++ -std=c++17 -Isrc test/flip_detector_test.cpp \
        src/domain/motion/flip_detector.cpp \
        -o .pio/flip_detector_test.exe
    .pio/flip_detector_test.exe

These tests cover wheel blocking, reverse rotation, slip evidence from GNSS,
gyro and fallback timing, and the 500 ms source-aware flip confirmation.

Magnetic calibration host simulation:

    g++ -std=c++17 -O2 -Isrc test/magnetic_calibrator_test.cpp \
        src/domain/localization/magnetic_calibrator.cpp \
        -o .pio/magnetic_calibrator_test.exe
    .pio/magnetic_calibrator_test.exe

The simulation rejects repeated poses, planar motion, tilted planar motion,
an upper hemisphere, and CAN samples. It also verifies an ideal full sphere
and a noisy full sphere after a long biased history, including fit accuracy.
